<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="d50bcf11">&lt;p&gt;
    It is quite common&amp;nbsp;for programs/projects to&amp;nbsp;realize additional unexpected benefits. These benefits whilst not
    captured through the benefits identification&amp;nbsp;task and therefore, not included in the business case, may still be
    important to the firm and should be tracked and reported once identified.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Some benefits are dynamic and might change or appear during the lifecycle of a program. Capture any ad-hoc benefits
    that arise from stakeholder discussions. Ad-hoc items tend to be personal experiences of users, and therefore add
    considerable credibility to the success of a program. Work with stakeholders to capture and formalize ad-hoc benefits
    in the benefits processes.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    It is also important to consider the opportunity for further benefits. New benefits should be considered that became
    available in the light of new levels of business performance that have been achieved.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
